[{"id":"VRCE-5-27-1","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":1,"english":"Like to servants waiting upon a wealthy person various auspicious omens waited upon Sītā of a blameless and comely person, racked with grief and divested of joy.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.845,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027001","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c6c6a13d96948fe2c5a3ed199bed82bf0d5118169a43e243668236bb4772ebf7","compact":"676a6ac7510fc8b77a65f4b561ceb7605bd97c90eb5be86d6299afdf4e60c252","nasal":"6a21b4fdf2e12eb009c217be6561cbd6948fffc272a582a820bfc38dedd9de8e"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-2","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":2,"english":"And the large left eye of that dame having graceful hairs, having dark pupils, white ends and thick eye-lashes, began to dance like a lotus shaken by a fish.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.703,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027002","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e7f676056f58e127ad048eb2088efbac17747d8b0a831adc1571444f1d69afac","compact":"51a7a585086af034e38a1d93eea47016736591d83c2212f33d7df5563998d644","nasal":"69da984891ce2916fc2b514696717bc787b9134e31fa684ad98d699e7531de57"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-3","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":3,"english":"And her beautiful, round, plump left hand, which, ere this, sprinkled with costly aguru and sandal, used to serve for a pillow to Rāma, began to dance now again and again after a long time.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.621,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027003","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4d295ec87af87621e906601c4414a3d3881716d85a632765beb6fa5a7a58f0cc","compact":"b9398d2e0005904ea31205e91c0ee9a01a6790ab0cc6ede834678ce181224bf9","nasal":"d5ddb978524f012101666d68d907b3a53406797842217b7859afceba0490f326"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-4","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":4,"english":"And her well built plump left thigh, resembling the trunk of an elephant, dancing, announced that she would soon behold Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.649,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027004","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cff505b4bbb05cb33ac464c6fd4037b1b84deb159ec64b08d698decf3e7ec428","compact":"3a77629271dd677f37cc67cd349923a7cdb52c17ef47201c59f58b1f111c029b","nasal":"01f29340bc7613f654b1adca554d4765ad3a6122352bf60389795a63e34ebb8a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-5","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":5,"english":"And the gold-hued cloth, now covered with dust, of that damsel of a comely person, having teeth like pomegranate seeds, slipped a little off its place.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.657,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027005","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"40b2eef99f32724c00e97510875f524f1d9f62f51a47e4b4d2dc2aa6ac885a53","compact":"8d61bcb41de4b102eb643955335dc7027a43d858ae4a505ffc853a9ddf2ccc86","nasal":"c1b995952e3f234ec16883fa70efb20375d69a1db4bcfdd30861cf3ca02cc981"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-6","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":6,"english":"And being enlivened by those and various other auspicious omens, she attained joy like to a seed, spoiled by the wind and the sun, growing afresh in the rainy season.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.804,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027006","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"85db5f0b409e7bdec3893f8e4afb8c707d8177e09a26e0cca3e27bf15f6c4a87","compact":"d00f9195ede08356a6d6f4d4103499b32517bf10bf24b91f33fbc9fdcdf02256","nasal":"2a4ed8904642af05877ba95b32b88bfafb74edff87d7bcbfc311a30162674d2f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-7","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":7,"english":"And her countenance, having lips red as Bimba fruits, beautiful eyes, eye-brows extending to ears, curling eye-lashes and white teeth, again appeared graceful like to the Moon released from the full grasp of Rahu. (Like the Moon when full eclipse is just over-In mythology Rāhu is the son of Sinika, a Daitya, with the tail of a dragon whose head was severed from-his body by Vişnu, but being immortal this head and tail retained their separate existence and being transferred to the stellar sphere, became the authors of eclipses.)","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.76,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027007","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d6c5f642bad208d11aeb48aa73cadb6008461e08258307e0e37044069e6c3e6d","compact":"1454d07575effc9c21d6ecc2cd26515ae0c486c631ec9fb54ea0bfb81a1fd903","nasal":"34e04b5a43bccac3a5028709cbd57b7edfc71415a1ae25bf9a0bec89f85f2271"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-27-8","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":27,"verse":8,"english":"Her grief and exhaustion were removed. sorrow was pacified and her heart was filled with joy. And she with her countenance appeared beautiful like a lunar night beautified by the rays of the Moon.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.811,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-29-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5027008","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerp